          },
          "code": "SHOW_DETAILS",
          "id": "1766562019626",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1766562019629_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Using data: {{$context.$nodes.TARGET_KEYTERM_DEVIATIONS.$output.result}} \nDerive `keyTermName` from: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.OraAction}} (extract the key term name string;).\n\nTask: Render the deviations as a Message List (messageListWidget).\n\nReturn ONLY the messageListWidget configuration object (no markdown/code fences, no extra text).\n\nOutput shape:\n- subtitle: keyTermName\n- items: one item per row in the input array\n\nFor each item:\n- title: contract identifier/name (contractNumber else RecordName else Contract else \"Unknown contract\")\n- subtitle: supplier name (supplierName )\n- summary: actual value (actualValue)\n- badgeText mapping:\n  - PROHIBITED → Avoid\n  - FALLBACK1 → Fallback 1\n  - FALLBACK2 → Fallback 2\n  - STANDARD → Standard\n  - otherwise → best-effort leading-cap label, else Unknown\n- priority mapping (must be one of: alert, warning, medium):\n  - PROHIBITED → alert\n  - FALLBACK1 → warning\n  - FALLBACK2 → warning\n  - STANDARD → medium\n  - missing/unknown → medium\n\nHard rules:\n- Do not output any dates anywhere (no timestamp; no date strings in title/subtitle/summary).\n- Do not include interactivity (no action, no additionalAction).\n- Do not include image."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "minimal"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "Gpt-5 Mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "Summarize"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "SUMMARIZE",
          "id": "1768331842692",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1768331842696_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Objective: Summarize the aggregated keyTerms array below in 5–6 crisp sentences for an executive audience.\nDo not repeat exact metrics (no percentages or supplier counts).\nFocus only on: the main risk areas and the priority action.\nKeep the tone direct and non-legalistic. Avoid bullet points and avoid copying table text verbatim.\nInsights must be written at a Grade 8 reading level.\nUse plain English, no legal jargon.\nOutput only the summary paragraph. \nNo need to mention the negotiation details.\n\n\n## Inputs (TWO inputs)\n### Input 1: keyTerms (array only; already aggregated; do NOT recompute metrics)\nYou will receive ONE JSON array. Each element includes:\n- `keyTermName` (string)\n- `deviationPercent` (number 0–100) ✅ already computed; DO NOT recalculate\n- `deviatingSuppliersCount` (number) ✅ already computed; DO NOT recalculate\n- `deviationCodeCounts` (object) e.g. `{ \"STANDARD\": 10, \"FALLBACK1\": 2, \"FALLBACK2\": 5, \"PROHIBITED\": 1 }`\n- `topExceptions` (array of `{ value: string, count: number }`) for deviating only; may be empty/missing\n- `deviations` (array, may be empty/missing): contract-level records for non-standard outcomes only, i.e. where `deviationCode` is `PROHIBITED`, `FALLBACK1`, or `FALLBACK2`\n- `standards` (array, may be empty/missing): contract-level records for standard outcomes only, i.e. where `deviationCode` is `STANDARD`\n\n### Input 2: rules (array)\nEach element describes the rule baseline for a key term:\n```json\n{\n  \"keyTermName\": \"<name>\",\n  \"standard\": \"<standard text>\",\n  \"fallbacks\": [ { \"name\": \"Fallback 1\", \"text\": \"...\" }, { \"name\": \"Fallback 2\", \"text\": \"...\" } ],\n  \"notAcceptable\": \"<not acceptable text>\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n## Output format (TEXT ONLY)\nReturn ONLY plain text.\n- Exactly **1 paragraph** \n- Total length: **<= 90 words**.\n\n---\n\nInterpretation labels (use in narrative text only)\n- PROHIBITED → “position to be avoided”\n- FALLBACK1 → fallback 1\n- FALLBACK2 → fallback 2\n- STANDARD → standard\nuse labels only when describing about classifications like PROHIBITED/FALLBACK1/FALLBACK2/STANDARD\n\n\n**Inputs:**  \nInput 1:  \n {{$context.$nodes.SUMMARIZED_JS.$output.result}}\nInput 2:  \n {{$context.$nodes.RULES_.$output.result}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [],
              "enableActions": false,
              "enableCommunications": true
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Generate Email"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GENERATE_EMAIL",
          "id": "1772119324114",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1772119324115_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Given this data: {{$context.$nodes.SUMMARIZED_JS.$output.result}}\n\nTask:\nGenerate a single customer-neutral, internal communication email draft summarizing contract key-term deviation pressure and recommended next steps, using ONLY the provided `keyTerms` aggregates and the optional `rules` for interpretation.\n\nInput shape (what you can use):\n- keyTerms[]:\n  - keyTermName\n  - deviationPercent (already computed; DO NOT recalculate)\n  - deviatingSuppliersCount (already computed; DO NOT recalculate)\n  - deviationCodeCounts { STANDARD, FALLBACK1, FALLBACK2, PROHIBITED }\n  - topExceptions[] (deviating only; may be empty)\n- rules[] (optional): standard / fallbacks / notAcceptable per keyTermName\n\nInterpretation labels (use these in narrative text only):\nWhen you refer to deviation codes in any generated text (including the summary paragraph, bullets, and any risk flags), use:\n- PROHIBITED → “position to be avoided”\n- FALLBACK1 → “fallback 1”\n- FALLBACK2 → “fallback 2”\n- STANDARD → “standard”\nImportant: Use the provided deviationCodeCounts keys (STANDARD/FALLBACK1/FALLBACK2/PROHIBITED) for any internal comparisons/logic, but render the labels above in the email text.\n\nNon-negotiable rules:\n- Do NOT recompute deviationPercent, unique supplier counts, or any frequency. Trust the input.\n- Do NOT add supplier names, contract numbers, or clause text unless explicitly present AND required (assume not required).\n- If topExceptions[0].value is long, do NOT paste it; summarize it in a few words (e.g., “delayed notification timelines”, “mutual indemnity language”).\n- Use rules ONLY to interpret what “standard vs fallback vs not acceptable” means at a high level; do not quote long rule text verbatim.\n- Prefer crisp, enterprise-appropriate language suitable for a screen-based email composer.\n- If keyTerms is empty or missing, output NOTHING (empty response) and no extra text.\n\nSelection & ordering logic (for content):\n- Identify the Top 3 terms by deviationPercent (descending). If fewer than 3, use what exists.\n- Identify “positions-to-be-avoided hotspots”: terms where deviationCodeCounts.PROHIBITED > 0. Mention count of such terms and name up to 3.\n- In next steps, prioritize terms with highest deviationPercent and any term with PROHIBITED presence.\n- To describe overall skew across the top terms: use the provided deviationCodeCounts for the selected top terms and determine whether the pattern is dominated by FALLBACK2 vs FALLBACK1 vs PROHIBITED (do not invent metrics; just compare the provided counts at a high level). In narrative text, describe the dominance using the interpretation labels (fallback 2 / fallback 1 / position(s) to be avoided).\n\nOutput format (NOT JSON; no markdown; email only):\nReturn exactly two parts in this order:\n1) A single subject line in plain text prefixed exactly as:\n   Subject: <your subject>\n2) Then the full email body as valid HTML only (no surrounding code fences), following the structure below.\n\nEmail body requirements (valid HTML only; use simple tags like <p>, <strong>, <ul>, <li>, <br>):\n- Greeting: “Hello team,”\n- 1 sentence context: reference “key-term deviation analysis” (do not reference dashboards or hidden calculations)\n- A crisp summary paragraph (2–3 sentences) that:\n  - names top 2 terms by deviationPercent with %\n  - states whether the pattern skews toward fallback 2 / fallback 1 / position(s) to be avoided based on deviationCodeCounts across the top terms\n  - includes a position-to-be-avoided risk flag if any exist\n- Section: \"<strong>Top terms under pressure</strong>\" then a <ul> with up to 3 <li> items:\n  - \"<Term>: <deviationPercent>% deviating across <deviatingSuppliersCount> suppliers (Position to be avoided present: Yes/No).\"\n  - Optionally add a very short “common exception theme” (do not paste long text)\n- Section: \"<strong>Proposed next steps</strong>\" then a <ul> with 3–5 action bullets, such as:\n  - tighten/standardize language for top term(s)\n  - address any positions-to-be-avoided hotspots aligned to “not acceptable” intent (using rules only at a high level)\n  - align negotiation guidance to fallback 1 / fallback 2 positioning\n  - confirm escalation/approval path for positions to be avoided outcomes\n- Closing CTA paragraph asking stakeholders to confirm priority focus areas and owners.\n- Signature:\n  Best regards,<br>{{SenderName}}\n  - SenderName: use the full name from {{$context.$app.$OraUserContext}}; if missing, use “TBD”.\n\nSubject line guidance:\n- Keep it concise and enterprise-appropriate.\n- Include “Key Term Deviations” and optionally mention positions to be avoided if present (e.g., “Key Term Deviations — Positions to Be Avoided Hotspots”)."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
