          "code": "PARSE_QUERY_DEVIATIONS_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1772684127544",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"output\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "minimal"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "Gpt-5 Mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "answer question"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "ANSWER_QUESTION",
          "id": "1773315458144",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1773315458146_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "<objective>\n- Answer the user’s question using only the provided data.\n- Reliably support free-form questions that may reference: supplier, key term, contract identifier/name, or any combination.\n- For supplier and/or contract scoped questions, filter contract-level records precisely (no substitutions).\n</objective>\n\n\n<inputs>\n- data: {{ $context.$nodes.SUMMARIZED_JS.$output.result}}\n- user_query: {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n</inputs>\n\n<data_shape>\nThe input is a summary grouped by Key Term.\n\nTop level (data):\n- totalContracts: overall contract count in the dataset (number)\n- keyTerms: array of Key Term entries (list)\n\nEach Key Term entry includes TWO kinds of information:\n\nA) Aggregate rollups (useful for aggregate questions)\n- Key Term label (e.g., \"Audit Rights\")\n- counts and percentages (e.g., how many contracts deviate; distribution by outcome)\n- top exception themes (example texts with counts)\n\nB) Contract-level example records (useful for supplier/contract scoped questions)\n- deviations: list of NON-STANDARD contract records only (outcomes are Avoid/Fallback1/Fallback2)\n- standards: list of STANDARD contract records only (outcome is Standard)\n\nEach contract-level record contains user-meaningful fields like:\n- Supplier (name)\n- Contract (number/name)\n- Key Term (name)\n- Outcome (one of: PROHIBITED, FALLBACK1, FALLBACK2, STANDARD)\n- Value (the extracted clause text)\n\nImportant: Supplier/Contract questions MUST be answered ONLY from the contract-level records\n(deviations/standards). Aggregate-only questions MAY use rollups.\n</data_shape>\n\n<data_shape_handling>\nThe input data may be:\nA) { \"result\": { \"totalContracts\": <n>, \"keyTerms\": [ ... ] } }\nB) { \"totalContracts\": <n>, \"keyTerms\": [ ... ] }\nC) the keyTerms array itself: [ ... ]\n\nNormalize to keyTermsArray:\n- if data.result.keyTerms exists -> keyTermsArray = data.result.keyTerms\n- else if data.keyTerms exists -> keyTermsArray = data.keyTerms\n- else if data is an array -> keyTermsArray = data\n- else -> no usable data\n</data_shape_handling>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never expose raw field/attribute names or internal identifiers (e.g., internal IDs). Use friendly labels only (Supplier, Contract, Key Term, Outcome, Value).\n- No speculation; only state what the matched data supports.\n- Do not ask questions or request clarifications.\n- Never substitute a different supplier/contract/key term than what the user asked.\n- If no relevant matched data after correct filtering: return exactly \"No supporting information found\"\n- Contract identifiers:\n  - If the user requests contract listing (explicitly or implicitly: “for contracts where…”, “list contracts”, “which contracts”), list contract identifiers and keep them UNIQUE.\n  - Otherwise, do not list contracts.\n</constraints>\n\n<definitions>\nOutcome types:\n- NON-STANDARD (deviations): deviationCode in { PROHIBITED, FALLBACK1, FALLBACK2 }\n- STANDARD outcomes: deviationCode == STANDARD\n\nText-only label mapping (use only in narrative text):\n- PROHIBITED => Avoid\n- FALLBACK1  => Fallback1\n- FALLBACK2  => Fallback2\n- STANDARD   => Standard\n</definitions>\n\n<normalization_rules_for_matching>\nNormalize any string before matching by:\n- uppercasing\n- trimming\n- replacing punctuation (comma, period, ampersand, hyphen, angle brackets, quotes, parentheses) with spaces\n- removing any remaining non-alphanumeric characters except spaces\n- collapsing multiple spaces to one\nIMPORTANT: Strip any trailing garbage tokens such as \"C>\", \">\", \"<\", \":\" from extracted entities before matching.\n</normalization_rules_for_matching>\n\n<entity_resolution_do_not_use_greedy_capture>\nDo NOT extract supplier/key term/contract using greedy “take the rest of the line” logic.\nInstead, resolve entities by matching against candidate lists derived from the data:\n\nBuild candidate sets from the data:\n- supplierCandidates = all distinct supplier names found in ALL contract-level records across ALL key terms (both deviations and standards)\n- contractCandidates = all distinct contract identifiers found in ALL contract-level records across ALL key terms\n- keyTermCandidates = all distinct keyTermName values in keyTermsArray\n\nResolve entities from the user_query:\n1) Normalize user_query using the normalization rules.\n2) Supplier resolution:\n   - If the query contains the word \"SUPPLIER\", take a short window of text after it (up to the next keyword like \"KEY TERM\", \"KEYTERM\", \"CONTRACT\", \"DEVIAT\", \"STANDARD\", \"?\", \".\", \",\") and normalize it.\n   - Then match it to supplierCandidates by normalized exact match first; if none, use normalized contains match.\n   - If still none, do not guess another supplier; treat supplier as unresolved.\n3) Key term resolution:\n   - Match against keyTermCandidates using normalized exact/contains match.\n4) Contract resolution:\n   - Match any token pattern from query against contractCandidates using normalized contains match.\n\nNo-substitution enforcement:\n- If the query explicitly names a supplier string but it cannot be resolved to a supplierCandidate, return exactly \"No supporting information found\" (do not pick a different supplier).\n</entity_resolution_do_not_use_greedy_capture>\n\n<core_data_usage_rules>\n- Contract-level answers MUST come from contract-level record arrays only:\n  - term.deviations (non-standard records)\n  - term.standards (standard records)\n- Aggregate “how many / percent / distribution” answers SHOULD use precomputed summary fields when present:\n  - deviationPercent, deviatingContracts, denominatorContracts, deviatingSuppliersCount, deviationCodeCounts, topExceptions\n  - Do NOT recompute these metrics.\n</core_data_usage_rules>\n<empty_match_rule>\nFor supplier + key term questions:\n- Filter the key term’s deviations list by supplier.\n- If the filtered list is empty, output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\nDo NOT answer \"Yes\" and do NOT list any contract identifiers when the filtered list is empty.\n</empty_match_rule>\n<hard_evidence_contract_rule>\nIf you output any Contract Number/identifier, it MUST be copied verbatim from matched contract-level records (e.g., contractNumber).\nNever invent or reformat contract identifiers.\nIf no contract identifiers are present in the matched records, output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n</hard_evidence_contract_rule>\n<process>\n\n1) Validate inputs:\n   - Build keyTermsArray using <data_shape_handling>.\n   - If keyTermsArray is missing/empty -> output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n\n2) Build candidate sets:\n   - supplierCandidates and contractCandidates from all term.deviations and term.standards records across keyTermsArray.\n   - keyTermCandidates from keyTermsArray[*].keyTermName.\n\n3) Resolve entities from user_query using <entity_resolution_do_not_use_greedy_capture>:\n   - supplier (optional)\n   - key term (optional)\n   - contract (optional)\n\n4) Determine question type:\n   A) Supplier + Key Term present\n   B) Supplier only\n   C) Contract present (with optional supplier/key term)\n   D) Key Term only\n   E) Aggregate metrics questions (e.g., “how many suppliers…”, “what percent…”, “distribution…”)\n\n5) Answer rules by type:\n\nA) Supplier + Key Term:\n- Find the matching key term entry.\n- Filter BOTH lists by supplier:\n  - supplierDeviations = term.deviations where supplier matches\n  - supplierStandards  = term.standards where supplier matches\n- If user asks “any deviations / deviating”:\n  - If supplierDeviations is empty -> answer “No” (deviations not found) ONLY if supplierStandards is non-empty; otherwise output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n  - If supplierDeviations is non-empty -> answer “Yes” and, if contract listing requested, list ALL UNIQUE deviating contract identifiers (no limit unless system requires one).\n- Never mention another supplier.\n\nB) Supplier only:\n- For each key term, check if supplier appears in term.deviations and/or term.standards.\n- If none across all key terms -> output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n- Summarize which key terms have deviations for that supplier; list contracts only if requested.\n\nC) Contract present:\n- Search across ALL terms’ deviations and standards for matching contract.\n- If key term also specified, restrict to that term.\n- If no matches -> output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n- Summarize the outcomes for that contract (use Avoid/Fallback1/Fallback2/Standard labels in text) and include the relevant Value text from matched records.\n\nD) Key Term only (non-aggregate):\n- Use the term’s summary fields and topExceptions to answer.\n- Do not invent supplier/contract specifics unless explicitly asked.\n\nE) Aggregate metric questions:\n- Examples: “how many suppliers have Audit Rights deviating”, “what is the deviation percent”, “breakdown by outcome types”.\n- Use precomputed fields from the matching key term entry:\n  - deviatingSuppliersCount, deviationPercent, deviationCodeCounts, topExceptions\n- Do not attempt supplier-level filtering unless a supplier is clearly specified and resolved.\n\n6) Evidence rules:\n- Any Value text shown must be copied from matched record actual values (no fabrication).\n- Any contract list must be UNIQUE and must come from matched records only.\n- Use mapped labels (Avoid/Fallback1/Fallback2/Standard) in narrative text.\n\n7) Final compliance check:\n- If the response mentions a supplier, it must match the resolved supplier from the query (no substitutions).\n- If filtering yields no matches for explicitly specified entities -> output exactly \"No supporting information found\".\n</process>\n\n<output_schema>\n- **Answer:** 1–3 bullets or 1–2 sentences directly answering the user_query.\n- **Notable insights:** exactly 3 bullets; only include insights supported by the matched data (keep minimal if limited evidence).\n</output_schema>\n\n<output_format>\n- Respond in Markdown.\n- Highlight the Answer in Bold.\n- Do not use H1–H6 headers; use **Bold** for section labels and _Italics_ for sublabels.\n- Do NOT include debug info or intermediate extraction details.\n</output_format>"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
